            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-31591",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: KVM: SEV: Lock all vCPUs when synchronzing VMSAs for SNP launch finish"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nKVM: SEV: Lock all vCPUs when synchronzing VMSAs for SNP launch finish\n\nLock all vCPUs when synchronizing and encrypting VMSAs for SNP guests, as\nallowing userspace to manipulate and/or run a vCPU while its state is being\nsynchronized would at best corrupt vCPU state, and at worst crash the host\nkernel.\n\nOpportunistically assert that vcpu-\u003emutex is held when synchronizing its\nVMSA (the SEV-ES path already locks vCPUs)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-31591",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-31591\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-31591](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-31591)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nKVM: SEV: Lock all vCPUs when synchronzing VMSAs for SNP launch finish\n\nLock all vCPUs when synchronizing and encrypting VMSAs for SNP guests, as\nallowing userspace to manipulate and/or run a vCPU while its state is being\nsynchronized would at best corrupt vCPU state, and at worst crash the host\nkernel.\n\nOpportunistically assert that vcpu-\u003emutex is held when synchronizing its\nVMSA (the SEV-ES path already locks vCPUs).",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-31591**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-31591](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-31591)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nKVM: SEV: Lock all vCPUs when synchronzing VMSAs for SNP launch finish\n\nLock all vCPUs when synchronizing and encrypting VMSAs for SNP guests, as\nallowing userspace to manipulate and/or run a vCPU while its state is being\nsynchronized would at best corrupt vCPU state, and at worst crash the host\nkernel.\n\nOpportunistically assert that vcpu-\u003emutex is held when synchronizing its\nVMSA (the SEV-ES path already locks vCPUs)."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cvssv3_baseScore": 5.5,
                "cvssv3_vector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H",
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
