            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-89503",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: ring-buffer: Fix subbuf resize race with ring_buffer_alloc_read_page()"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nring-buffer: Fix subbuf resize race with ring_buffer_alloc_read_page()\n\nring_buffer_alloc_read_page() is racy with ring_buffer_subbuf_order_set,\nit can allocate a reader page with an outdated order. This isn't a big\nissue, the user can still re-allocate a new reader page and try again.\n\nHowever, what is more problematic is if the value of subbuf_order\nchanges in the middle of ring_buffer_alloc_read_page(). In that case,\nbpage-\u003eorder might not match the actual allocated memory.\n\nUse bpage-\u003eorder for the allocation to prevent this race."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-89503",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-89503\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-89503](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-89503)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nring-buffer: Fix subbuf resize race with ring_buffer_alloc_read_page()\n\nring_buffer_alloc_read_page() is racy with ring_buffer_subbuf_order_set,\nit can allocate a reader page with an outdated order. This isn't a big\nissue, the user can still re-allocate a new reader page and try again.\n\nHowever, what is more problematic is if the value of subbuf_order\nchanges in the middle of ring_buffer_alloc_read_page(). In that case,\nbpage-\u003eorder might not match the actual allocated memory.\n\nUse bpage-\u003eorder for the allocation to prevent this race.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-89503**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-89503](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-89503)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nring-buffer: Fix subbuf resize race with ring_buffer_alloc_read_page()\n\nring_buffer_alloc_read_page() is racy with ring_buffer_subbuf_order_set,\nit can allocate a reader page with an outdated order. This isn't a big\nissue, the user can still re-allocate a new reader page and try again.\n\nHowever, what is more problematic is if the value of subbuf_order\nchanges in the middle of ring_buffer_alloc_read_page(). In that case,\nbpage-\u003eorder might not match the actual allocated memory.\n\nUse bpage-\u003eorder for the allocation to prevent this race."
              },
