            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-97536",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: scsi: qla2xxx: Fix use-after-free of qpair work on queue teardown"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nscsi: qla2xxx: Fix use-after-free of qpair work on queue teardown\n\nThe response queue MSI-X handler qla2xxx_msix_rsp_q() schedules\nqla_do_work() via queue_work(ha-\u003ewq, \u0026qpair-\u003eq_work). qla_do_work()\ndereferences the qpair (vha, rsp) and takes qpair-\u003eqp_lock.\n\nDuring teardown, qla2xxx_delete_qpair() deletes the response queue, which\ncalls free_irq() in qla25xx_free_rsp_que(), and then frees the queue and\nthe qpair. free_irq() waits for running hardirq handlers but does not\ncancel work already placed on ha-\u003ewq. A still-pending q_work then runs\nqla_do_work() against the freed qpair and response queue, causing a\nuse-after-free. This is especially likely during full adapter teardown,\nwhere destroy_workqueue(ha-\u003ewq) forces pending work to run after the queue\npairs have been freed.\n\nFlush the work item with cancel_work_sync() in qla25xx_free_rsp_que()\nafter free_irq() has released the interrupt (so no new work can be\nqueued) and before the response queue and qpair memory are freed (so the\nflushed handler still sees valid memory). Guard on rsp-\u003eqpair and ha-\u003ewq\nto match the INIT_WORK() condition and avoid operating on an\nuninitialized work_struct."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97536",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-97536\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-97536](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97536)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nscsi: qla2xxx: Fix use-after-free of qpair work on queue teardown\n\nThe response queue MSI-X handler qla2xxx_msix_rsp_q() schedules\nqla_do_work() via queue_work(ha-\u003ewq, \u0026qpair-\u003eq_work). qla_do_work()\ndereferences the qpair (vha, rsp) and takes qpair-\u003eqp_lock.\n\nDuring teardown, qla2xxx_delete_qpair() deletes the response queue, which\ncalls free_irq() in qla25xx_free_rsp_que(), and then frees the queue and\nthe qpair. free_irq() waits for running hardirq handlers but does not\ncancel work already placed on ha-\u003ewq. A still-pending q_work then runs\nqla_do_work() against the freed qpair and response queue, causing a\nuse-after-free. This is especially likely during full adapter teardown,\nwhere destroy_workqueue(ha-\u003ewq) forces pending work to run after the queue\npairs have been freed.\n\nFlush the work item with cancel_work_sync() in qla25xx_free_rsp_que()\nafter free_irq() has released the interrupt (so no new work can be\nqueued) and before the response queue and qpair memory are freed (so the\nflushed handler still sees valid memory). Guard on rsp-\u003eqpair and ha-\u003ewq\nto match the INIT_WORK() condition and avoid operating on an\nuninitialized work_struct.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-97536**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-97536](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97536)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nscsi: qla2xxx: Fix use-after-free of qpair work on queue teardown\n\nThe response queue MSI-X handler qla2xxx_msix_rsp_q() schedules\nqla_do_work() via queue_work(ha-\u003ewq, \u0026qpair-\u003eq_work). qla_do_work()\ndereferences the qpair (vha, rsp) and takes qpair-\u003eqp_lock.\n\nDuring teardown, qla2xxx_delete_qpair() deletes the response queue, which\ncalls free_irq() in qla25xx_free_rsp_que(), and then frees the queue and\nthe qpair. free_irq() waits for running hardirq handlers but does not\ncancel work already placed on ha-\u003ewq. A still-pending q_work then runs\nqla_do_work() against the freed qpair and response queue, causing a\nuse-after-free. This is especially likely during full adapter teardown,\nwhere destroy_workqueue(ha-\u003ewq) forces pending work to run after the queue\npairs have been freed.\n\nFlush the work item with cancel_work_sync() in qla25xx_free_rsp_que()\nafter free_irq() has released the interrupt (so no new work can be\nqueued) and before the response queue and qpair memory are freed (so the\nflushed handler still sees valid memory). Guard on rsp-\u003eqpair and ha-\u003ewq\nto match the INIT_WORK() condition and avoid operating on an\nuninitialized work_struct."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-97537",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: scsi: qla2xxx: Fix queue teardown NULL dma_free and bitmap locking"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nscsi: qla2xxx: Fix queue teardown NULL dma_free and bitmap locking\n\nqla25xx_free_req_que() and qla25xx_free_rsp_que() have two pre-existing\nbugs exposed on the error path of qla25xx_create_{req,rsp}_que():\n\n 1. When dma_alloc_coherent() fails during queue creation, the error\n    path calls the free function with req-\u003ering / rsp-\u003ering still NULL\n    (from kzalloc).  The unconditional dma_free_coherent() with a NULL\n    cpu_addr is undefined behavior and can panic.\n\n 2. The free functions clear req_qid_map / rsp_qid_map under vport_lock,\n    but the create functions protect the same bitmaps with mq_lock.\n    This provides no mutual exclusion.  Additionally, the create error\n    path clears the bit and releases mq_lock before calling the free\n    function, creating a window where another thread can allocate the\n    same que_id and have its ha-\u003ereq_q_map entry clobbered by the\n    subsequent lockless NULL assignment in the free function.\n\nFix by:\n\n - Guarding dma_free_coherent() with a NULL check on the ring pointer.\n\n - Using mq_lock (the lock held by all creators) in the free functions\n   to atomically NULL the map entry and clear the bitmap bit.\n\n - Removing the now-redundant clear_bit blocks from the create error\n   paths since the free functions handle it atomically."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97537",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-97537\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-97537](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97537)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nscsi: qla2xxx: Fix queue teardown NULL dma_free and bitmap locking\n\nqla25xx_free_req_que() and qla25xx_free_rsp_que() have two pre-existing\nbugs exposed on the error path of qla25xx_create_{req,rsp}_que():\n\n 1. When dma_alloc_coherent() fails during queue creation, the error\n    path calls the free function with req-\u003ering / rsp-\u003ering still NULL\n    (from kzalloc).  The unconditional dma_free_coherent() with a NULL\n    cpu_addr is undefined behavior and can panic.\n\n 2. The free functions clear req_qid_map / rsp_qid_map under vport_lock,\n    but the create functions protect the same bitmaps with mq_lock.\n    This provides no mutual exclusion.  Additionally, the create error\n    path clears the bit and releases mq_lock before calling the free\n    function, creating a window where another thread can allocate the\n    same que_id and have its ha-\u003ereq_q_map entry clobbered by the\n    subsequent lockless NULL assignment in the free function.\n\nFix by:\n\n - Guarding dma_free_coherent() with a NULL check on the ring pointer.\n\n - Using mq_lock (the lock held by all creators) in the free functions\n   to atomically NULL the map entry and clear the bitmap bit.\n\n - Removing the now-redundant clear_bit blocks from the create error\n   paths since the free functions handle it atomically.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-97537**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-97537](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97537)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nscsi: qla2xxx: Fix queue teardown NULL dma_free and bitmap locking\n\nqla25xx_free_req_que() and qla25xx_free_rsp_que() have two pre-existing\nbugs exposed on the error path of qla25xx_create_{req,rsp}_que():\n\n 1. When dma_alloc_coherent() fails during queue creation, the error\n    path calls the free function with req-\u003ering / rsp-\u003ering still NULL\n    (from kzalloc).  The unconditional dma_free_coherent() with a NULL\n    cpu_addr is undefined behavior and can panic.\n\n 2. The free functions clear req_qid_map / rsp_qid_map under vport_lock,\n    but the create functions protect the same bitmaps with mq_lock.\n    This provides no mutual exclusion.  Additionally, the create error\n    path clears the bit and releases mq_lock before calling the free\n    function, creating a window where another thread can allocate the\n    same que_id and have its ha-\u003ereq_q_map entry clobbered by the\n    subsequent lockless NULL assignment in the free function.\n\nFix by:\n\n - Guarding dma_free_coherent() with a NULL check on the ring pointer.\n\n - Using mq_lock (the lock held by all creators) in the free functions\n   to atomically NULL the map entry and clear the bitmap bit.\n\n - Removing the now-redundant clear_bit blocks from the create error\n   paths since the free functions handle it atomically."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-97538",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: hwmon: (asus_rog_ryujin) Validate HID report lengths"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nhwmon: (asus_rog_ryujin) Validate HID report lengths\n\nrog_ryujin_raw_event() parses response headers and payload fields without\nfirst checking that they are present in the received report. A short report\ncan therefore make the driver consume uninitialized bytes from the HID\ntransport buffer and expose them as sensor values through sysfs.\n\nValidate the response header and the fields used by each response type\nbefore parsing them."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97538",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-97538\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-97538](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97538)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nhwmon: (asus_rog_ryujin) Validate HID report lengths\n\nrog_ryujin_raw_event() parses response headers and payload fields without\nfirst checking that they are present in the received report. A short report\ncan therefore make the driver consume uninitialized bytes from the HID\ntransport buffer and expose them as sensor values through sysfs.\n\nValidate the response header and the fields used by each response type\nbefore parsing them.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-97538**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-97538](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97538)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nhwmon: (asus_rog_ryujin) Validate HID report lengths\n\nrog_ryujin_raw_event() parses response headers and payload fields without\nfirst checking that they are present in the received report. A short report\ncan therefore make the driver consume uninitialized bytes from the HID\ntransport buffer and expose them as sensor values through sysfs.\n\nValidate the response header and the fields used by each response type\nbefore parsing them."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
