            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-97938",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: reboot: fix cad_pid use-after-free race"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nreboot: fix cad_pid use-after-free race\n\ncad_pid is a single kernel-wide struct pid pointer. proc_do_cad_pid()\nreads it and passes it to pid_vnr() without protecting the lifetime of\nthe referenced struct pid. A concurrent writer can replace cad_pid and\ndrop the final reference to the old struct pid after the reader has\nloaded the pointer but before pid_vnr() has finished dereferencing it,\ncausing a use-after-free.\n\nkill_cad_pid() has the same lifetime race when it passes cad_pid to\nkill_pid().\n\nAt the time this issue was reported, an unprivileged user could reach the\nsysctl through user and PID namespaces because cad_pid was registered in\npid_table[]. Moving cad_pid back to the global reboot sysctl table\ncorrected that namespace and permission mismatch, but did not fix the\nunderlying lifetime race.\n\nFix this by treating cad_pid as an RCU-protected pointer at both read\nsites and by waiting for a grace period before dropping the old reference\non the write side.\n\ncall_rcu(\u0026old_pid-\u003ercu, ...) cannot be used here because free_pid()\nalso queues pid-\u003ercu; queueing the same rcu_head twice can corrupt the\nRCU callback list.\n\nOriginal KASAN crash stack:\n  kernel/pid.c:545 pid_nr_ns()        # reads freed pid-\u003elevel\n  kernel/pid.c:556 pid_vnr()          # calls pid_nr_ns()\n  kernel/pid.c:775 proc_do_cad_pid()  # calls pid_vnr(cad_pid)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97938",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-97938\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-97938](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97938)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nreboot: fix cad_pid use-after-free race\n\ncad_pid is a single kernel-wide struct pid pointer. proc_do_cad_pid()\nreads it and passes it to pid_vnr() without protecting the lifetime of\nthe referenced struct pid. A concurrent writer can replace cad_pid and\ndrop the final reference to the old struct pid after the reader has\nloaded the pointer but before pid_vnr() has finished dereferencing it,\ncausing a use-after-free.\n\nkill_cad_pid() has the same lifetime race when it passes cad_pid to\nkill_pid().\n\nAt the time this issue was reported, an unprivileged user could reach the\nsysctl through user and PID namespaces because cad_pid was registered in\npid_table[]. Moving cad_pid back to the global reboot sysctl table\ncorrected that namespace and permission mismatch, but did not fix the\nunderlying lifetime race.\n\nFix this by treating cad_pid as an RCU-protected pointer at both read\nsites and by waiting for a grace period before dropping the old reference\non the write side.\n\ncall_rcu(\u0026old_pid-\u003ercu, ...) cannot be used here because free_pid()\nalso queues pid-\u003ercu; queueing the same rcu_head twice can corrupt the\nRCU callback list.\n\nOriginal KASAN crash stack:\n  kernel/pid.c:545 pid_nr_ns()        # reads freed pid-\u003elevel\n  kernel/pid.c:556 pid_vnr()          # calls pid_nr_ns()\n  kernel/pid.c:775 proc_do_cad_pid()  # calls pid_vnr(cad_pid)",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-97938**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-97938](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97938)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nreboot: fix cad_pid use-after-free race\n\ncad_pid is a single kernel-wide struct pid pointer. proc_do_cad_pid()\nreads it and passes it to pid_vnr() without protecting the lifetime of\nthe referenced struct pid. A concurrent writer can replace cad_pid and\ndrop the final reference to the old struct pid after the reader has\nloaded the pointer but before pid_vnr() has finished dereferencing it,\ncausing a use-after-free.\n\nkill_cad_pid() has the same lifetime race when it passes cad_pid to\nkill_pid().\n\nAt the time this issue was reported, an unprivileged user could reach the\nsysctl through user and PID namespaces because cad_pid was registered in\npid_table[]. Moving cad_pid back to the global reboot sysctl table\ncorrected that namespace and permission mismatch, but did not fix the\nunderlying lifetime race.\n\nFix this by treating cad_pid as an RCU-protected pointer at both read\nsites and by waiting for a grace period before dropping the old reference\non the write side.\n\ncall_rcu(\u0026old_pid-\u003ercu, ...) cannot be used here because free_pid()\nalso queues pid-\u003ercu; queueing the same rcu_head twice can corrupt the\nRCU callback list.\n\nOriginal KASAN crash stack:\n  kernel/pid.c:545 pid_nr_ns()        # reads freed pid-\u003elevel\n  kernel/pid.c:556 pid_vnr()          # calls pid_nr_ns()\n  kernel/pid.c:775 proc_do_cad_pid()  # calls pid_vnr(cad_pid)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-97939",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: ipmr: account multicast table and route memory"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nipmr: account multicast table and route memory\n\nA netadmin in a user+net namespace can create many IPv4 and IPv6\nmulticast routing tables with MRT_TABLE and MRT6_TABLE. Each unseen\nid allocates an mr_table via the shared mr_table_alloc(), links it\ninto the per-net list, and leaves it until netns teardown. Those\nobjects were not charged to memcg, so the host unreclaimable slab\ngrows with the table count.\n\nAccount mr_table allocations with GFP_KERNEL_ACCOUNT and mark the\nIPv4/IPv6 MFC caches SLAB_ACCOUNT. This matches the established\nhandling of IP addresses, routes and alternate interface names.\n\nUnresolved MFC entries are still allocated from softIRQ with\nGFP_ATOMIC and are not charged. They expire after 10 seconds and are\nbounded by the socket receive queue; see commit 0079ad8e8dc3\n(\"ipmr: remove hard code cache_resolve_queue_len limit\")."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97939",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-97939\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-97939](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97939)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nipmr: account multicast table and route memory\n\nA netadmin in a user+net namespace can create many IPv4 and IPv6\nmulticast routing tables with MRT_TABLE and MRT6_TABLE. Each unseen\nid allocates an mr_table via the shared mr_table_alloc(), links it\ninto the per-net list, and leaves it until netns teardown. Those\nobjects were not charged to memcg, so the host unreclaimable slab\ngrows with the table count.\n\nAccount mr_table allocations with GFP_KERNEL_ACCOUNT and mark the\nIPv4/IPv6 MFC caches SLAB_ACCOUNT. This matches the established\nhandling of IP addresses, routes and alternate interface names.\n\nUnresolved MFC entries are still allocated from softIRQ with\nGFP_ATOMIC and are not charged. They expire after 10 seconds and are\nbounded by the socket receive queue; see commit 0079ad8e8dc3\n(\"ipmr: remove hard code cache_resolve_queue_len limit\").",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-97939**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-97939](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97939)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nipmr: account multicast table and route memory\n\nA netadmin in a user+net namespace can create many IPv4 and IPv6\nmulticast routing tables with MRT_TABLE and MRT6_TABLE. Each unseen\nid allocates an mr_table via the shared mr_table_alloc(), links it\ninto the per-net list, and leaves it until netns teardown. Those\nobjects were not charged to memcg, so the host unreclaimable slab\ngrows with the table count.\n\nAccount mr_table allocations with GFP_KERNEL_ACCOUNT and mark the\nIPv4/IPv6 MFC caches SLAB_ACCOUNT. This matches the established\nhandling of IP addresses, routes and alternate interface names.\n\nUnresolved MFC entries are still allocated from softIRQ with\nGFP_ATOMIC and are not charged. They expire after 10 seconds and are\nbounded by the socket receive queue; see commit 0079ad8e8dc3\n(\"ipmr: remove hard code cache_resolve_queue_len limit\")."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
